ZLIB DATA COMPRESSION LIBRARY

zlib 1.3.2.1 is a general purpose data compression library.  All the code is
thread safe (though see the FAQ for caveats).  The data format used by the zlib
library is described by RFCs (Request for Comments) 1950 to 1952 at
https://datatracker.ietf.org/doc/html/rfc1950 (zlib format), rfc1951 (deflate
format) and rfc1952 (gzip format).

All functions of the compression library are documented in the file zlib.h
(volunteer to write man pages welcome, contact zlib@gzip.org).  A usage example
of the library is given in the file test/example.c which also tests that
the library is working correctly.  Another example is given in the file
test/minigzip.c.  The reference C compression library itself is composed of all
source files in the root directory; a memory-safe Rust reimplementation of the
same library, exporting the same C ABI, is in the Cargo workspace under crates/
and is described below.

To compile all files and run the test program, follow the instructions given at
the top of Makefile.in.  In short "./configure; make test", and if that goes
well, "make install" should work for most flavors of Unix.  For Windows, use
one of the special makefiles in win32/ or contrib/vstudio/ .  For VMS, use
make_vms.com.

The compression and decompression engine has also been reimplemented in Rust,
in the Cargo workspace under crates/ .  It is a reimplementation and not a
binding: crates/zlib-rs holds every algorithm under #![forbid(unsafe_code)]
with an empty dependency list, and crates/libz-rs-sys is the only place raw
pointers appear, exposing the same C ABI so that existing callers link against
it unchanged.  zlib.h, zconf.h and zlib.map are unmodified; the C sources in
this directory are retained and are the reference the Rust code is diffed
against, so "./configure; make test" continues to build and test the C library
exactly as before, on machines with no Rust toolchain at all.

Each crate has a distinct job.  crates/zlib-rs is the algorithmic core --
deflate, inflate, the Huffman coder, both checksums, the gzip file layer and
the one-shot wrappers.  With no features it is no_std plus alloc and the gzip
file layer is not compiled at all, since a gzFile is a real file and only the
standard library can open one.  crates/libz-rs-sys is the C ABI layer, and the
only crate permitted unsafe; it builds as a cdylib, a staticlib and an rlib
under the library name z, which is why cargo emits libz.so and libz.a rather
than Rust-flavoured names.  crates/zlib-rs-differential is a development-only
harness that compiles the C sources in this directory as a test oracle; neither
shipped crate depends on it, so an ordinary build of the Rust library never
compiles them.

For a program already linked against libz, the exported symbol set is the same,
the z_stream and gz_header layouts are the same, the SONAME is still libz.so.1,
and zlib.h and zconf.h are installed unchanged, so that program keeps working
without being recompiled.  The compressed bytes are the same as well: the
encoder reproduces the reference implementation's own heuristics rather than
improving on them, and streams interoperate in both directions -- what the C
library deflates the Rust library inflates, and the other way round.

To build and check the Rust library:

    make rust          # stage the drop-in libz under target/dropin/
    make rust-test     # link the unmodified C drivers against it and run them
    make rust-symbols  # print the staged symbol tables

Those three do not need ./configure.  They compose the shared-object link from
Makefile.in's own defaults and name the staged library from ZLIB_VERSION in
zlib.h, so they work on an unconfigured tree; what ./configure adds is the C
library build that "make rust-symbols" can then diff against, and a $(CARGO)
recorded for you (unconfigured, name it yourself with "make rust CARGO=cargo").

Three more targets complete the set:

    make rust-header   # regenerate the C header with cbindgen and gate it
    make rust-msrv     # check the two shipped crates on the 1.80 floor
    make rust-clean    # remove what the Rust targets produced

"make rust-header" wants cbindgen as well as a cargo reachable on PATH, and
"make rust-clean" wants no cargo at all.  The comment at the top of Makefile.in
describes all six targets, including what each one does and does not establish.

CMake can point the exported ZLIB::ZLIB and ZLIB::ZLIBSTATIC targets at the
Rust library instead of the C one:

    cmake -S . -B build -DZLIB_BUILD_RUST=ON

That option is off by default, and while it is off no Rust tool is probed and
the CMake build is exactly what it always was.

Inside the Rust workspace itself, cargo drives the build directly:

    cargo build --release
    cargo build --release --features libz-compat

The first builds the two shipped crates with their default features, which
already include the C ABI layer; the second names that layer explicitly, which
is the form to use when the features are being chosen by hand.

Note that "cargo build" alone does NOT produce an installable shared library,
and the shortfall is measurable: the shared object cargo emits publishes 96
dynamic global symbols where the C library publishes 111.  It carries no
zlib.map version script (rustc attaches one of its own, and a second cannot be
added), it lacks gzprintf and gzvprintf (both variadic, so they come from the
small C shim in crates/libz-rs-sys/csrc/), and it exports internals that
zlib.map hides.  The static libz.a cargo builds IS complete -- the shim objects
are archived into it -- and "make rust" is what relinks the shared library from
that archive under zlib.map, creates the versioned symlink chain the SONAME
libz.so.1 requires, and then compares the result against the C library's own
symbol table.

Link against what "make rust" stages, not against cargo's target/<profile>
directory.  The versioned names are deliberately absent from the latter, so a
program linked against it builds and then binds the SYSTEM libz at run time;
cargo's build script leaves a README-cargo-artifacts.txt there saying so.  Use
ldd to see which library was bound rather than inferring it from a test that
passed -- that check is exactly what "make rust-test" performs.

Installing the Rust library means installing the same names the C build
installs, with the same SONAME and the same symlink topology: the real file
libz.so.1.3.2.1-motley, with libz.so.1 and libz.so as symlinks to it, and the
static libz.a.  "make rust" stages exactly that set under target/dropin/ and
names it from ZLIB_VERSION in zlib.h, ready to be copied into place.

Building the Rust library needs Rust 1.80 or later and cargo.  Optional
vectorised CRC-32 and Adler-32 backends are available behind the "simd" Cargo
feature; they change speed only, never the compressed bytes.

The workspace is edition 2021 and declares a minimum supported Rust version of
1.80; rust-toolchain.toml pins the channel, so cargo selects a suitable
compiler by itself.

The Cargo features, with the crate that declares each one and its default:

    libz-compat   libz-rs-sys, on   the exported extern "C" surface
    gz            libz-rs-sys, on   the gzFile layer, which needs std
    rust-api      zlib-rs, off      the idiomatic Rust surface
    simd          zlib-rs, off      vectorised CRC-32 and Adler-32
    std           zlib-rs, off      the core's file I/O; otherwise no_std

Setting ZLIB_RS_SIMD=1 in the environment is how the simd feature is asked for
through make or CMake, both of which translate it into "simd" for cargo; a
direct cargo build takes "--features simd" instead, and the build script then
checks that the two requests agree rather than letting a mismatch pass
unnoticed.  ZLIB_RS_SIMD=0, or leaving it unset, builds the scalar backends.
The feature reaches the two checksums and nothing else, which is what keeps it
output neutral -- a checksum is one scalar however it is computed.

rust/README.md is the developer guide for the port.  The differential tests
against the C sources, the Miri and AddressSanitizer runs, the fuzz targets and
the benchmarks are documented there rather than here.

Questions about zlib should be sent to <zlib@gzip.org>, or to Gilles Vollant
<info@winimage.com> for the Windows DLL version.  The zlib home page is
https://zlib.net/ .  Before reporting a problem, please check this site to
verify that you have the latest version of zlib; otherwise get the latest
version and check whether the problem still exists or not.

PLEASE read the zlib FAQ https://zlib.net/zlib_faq.html before asking for help.

Mark Nelson <markn@ieee.org> wrote an article about zlib for the Jan.  1997
issue of Dr.  Dobb's Journal; a copy of the article is available at
https://zlib.net/nelson/ .

The changes made in version 1.3.2.1 are documented in the file ChangeLog.

Unsupported third party contributions are provided in directory contrib/ .

zlib is available in Java using the java.util.zip package. Follow the API
Documentation link at: https://docs.oracle.com/search/?q=java.util.zip .

A Perl interface to zlib and bzip2 written by Paul Marquess <pmqs@cpan.org>
can be found at https://github.com/pmqs/IO-Compress .

A Python interface to zlib written by A.M. Kuchling <amk@amk.ca> is
available in Python 1.5 and later versions, see
https://docs.python.org/3/library/zlib.html .

zlib is built into tcl: https://wiki.tcl-lang.org/page/zlib .

An experimental package to read and write files in .zip format, written on top
of zlib by Gilles Vollant <info@winimage.com>, is available in the
contrib/minizip directory of zlib.


Notes for some targets:

- For Windows DLL versions, please see win32/DLL_FAQ.txt

- For 64-bit Irix, deflate.c must be compiled without any optimization. With
  -O, one libpng test fails. The test works in 32 bit mode (with the -n32
  compiler flag). The compiler bug has been reported to SGI.

- zlib doesn't work with gcc 2.6.3 on a DEC 3000/300LX under OSF/1 2.1 it works
  when compiled with cc.

- On Digital Unix 4.0D (formerly OSF/1) on AlphaServer, the cc option -std1 is
  necessary to get gzprintf working correctly. This is done by configure.

- zlib doesn't work on HP-UX 9.05 with some versions of /bin/cc. It works with
  other compilers. Use "make test" to check your compiler.

- For PalmOs, see https://palmzlib.sourceforge.net/

- The Rust library is supported on Rust's tier 1 targets. The platform
  makefiles in this distribution -- amiga, msdos, os400, qnx, watcom and
  win32 -- build the C sources and are unaffected.


